omic series the long history  of the necrosword its true nature as a  living symbiote and its creator the dark  god null have been fully fleshed out but  throughout the weapons mcu debut and  thor love and thunder the weapon is  never explained satisfactorily it is  evident from the fact that every god  seems aware of and afraid of the necro  sword that the weapon's history is long  in story but no portion of that story is  ever revealed when we first see the  sword it lays motionless next to the  body of its former wielder an unnamed  dead god beyond that we never learned  much about the sword aside from its  habit of slowly poisoning and maddening  its wielder as of a post-credits scene  in spider-man no way home at least the  venom symbiote does indeed exist in the  mcu proper so it's possible that a more  explicit connection to symbiotes will be  revealed in the future  okay yes the real world answer to the  question of sip's whereabouts since the  events of thor the dark world has an  easy enough answer the actor that  portrays sif jamie alexander was busy  filming her hit nbc crime drama series  blind spot but every hollywood  production is limited by availability  budget and an endless amount of other  constraints and those films especially  if they're in a connected universe like  the mcu need an in-canon explanation as  well lady siff was absent from the big  screen for nine years and according to  thor love and thunder the asgardian  warrior was out there somewhere fighting  something for some reason when we catch  up with sith via an interstellar  distress signal in thor love and thunder  she's in the midst of combat fighting  alongside some unnamed alien gods  against gore and losing badly however  that small bit of information is where  the statements end and the questions  begin where was sif all this time was  she not on asgard when it fell if not  why why didn't she return to new asgard  to help her people rebuild their  civilization who were the gods she  fought with and how did she know about  gore well in advance of thor and the  guardians of the galaxy  in the mcu for some reason it seems that  earth's gods are much more important to  the cosmos and its many goings on than  any other planets just like sif's  absence there is a simple real world  explanation for this earth bias the  people who make and watch the movies are  all from earth and while that certainly  explains why we watch movies about thor  instead of movies about some random god  from another random rock in space thor  love and thunder added a couple of  remarkable details to the mcu that make  earth's central status a bit more  baffling firstly omnipotence city the  meeting place of all the universes or at  least galaxies many pantheons is ruled  over by zeus of the earth-based greek  pantheon given that nasa estimates our  galaxy to contain over 100 billion  planets the odds that a god from earth  would be in charge are well about 1 in  100 billion secondly the film's second  half centers around gore's attempts to  reach the abstract cosmic entity known  as eternity and for some reason the  bifrost is the one key to getting there  for some reason earth's norse pantheon  and specifically its god of thunder  contains the sole means of access to  eternity surely one of the other 100  billion planets has a more suitable  pantheon and more suitable god for  getting to eternity  earth gods aren't the only beings whose  status in the cosmic order is in  question as of thor love and thunder its  regular human status is two  when daily life in new asgard is finally  revealed in greater detail we see that  it employs its own police force which is  equipped with armor and weapons that  look purposely half-human and half-ass  guardian that suggests an intermingling  of technology just as their flying tour  boats do given the fact that at least  some humans now have access to asgardian  technology in the story and since the  entire plot of black panther revolves  around the hyper-advanced country  finally sharing its technology with the  rest of the world as well we can only  assume that the humans of the mcu are  now centuries more advanced than we are  and there's another major asset that the  mcu humans have that we don't dr jane  foster when explaining her book and the  research behind it foster reveals that  she has essentially solved the physics  behind einstein rosenbridges aka  wormholes given that many physicists  believe that wormholes are based on laws  from both general relativity and quantum  mechanics foster's findings heavily  imply that she has at least partially  cracked the theory of everything slash  grand unifying theory even being  conservative foster's theoretical  advances paired with newly accessible  wakandan and asgardian tech means that  the humans of earth 616 have a bright  future close at hand in both the marvel  comics and the mcu the awesome night  infinite power of odin is named the odin  force it's one of the strongest and most  versatile energy sources in the universe  making odin more fearsome and formidable  than nearly any other god and even as  much as some cosmic entities like  galactus the odin force is what allows  odin to empower his spear gungner the  destroyer and even thor's trusty mjolnir  so it's clearly a force to be reckoned  with that's why it's absence from the  mcu after odin's death is not only  noticeable but raises the question wait  where did it go  in the comics the odin forces passed  down from king to king thor wielded the  bore force and then passed it to odin as  the odin force and he in turn passed it  to thor as the thor force but the mcu  never explains nor shows this and that  leads to two all-important questions  first did thor ever inherit that  tremendous power and if not might  instead the power have passed to  asgard's king valkyrie in the past the  line of kings has always been genetic as  well as political but ever since the end  of avengers end game when thor made  valkyrie king the two lines are separate  and so the mcu may very well have its  own thor force valkyrie force both or  neither  if there is one question that the marvel  movies of phase 4 have repeatedly raised  it is to what extent are humanity power  and destiny all related in spider-man no  way home we saw a human doctor strange  tear apart the fabric of the multiverse  in doctor strange in the multiverse of  madness we saw both strange and another  human the scarlet witch do the same on  an even larger scale  then in thor love and thunder we see  another human jane foster wield the  weapon of a god become a god herself and  fight toe to toe with gods as did her  antagonist gore and with the increasing  number of uber-powerful beings  populating the mcu that are not gods  like the watcher and the celestials the  question of what exactly a god is has  become more vaccine than ever a  particularly puzzling detail about the  mcu's divine beings is that their  continued existence and power don't seem  to be connected to the one thing you'd  expect to be guaranteed worship when a  desperate broken gore comes across his  own god and explains that all of his  worshippers are dead god admits that he  is happy to see them go he doesn't need  them nor does he care about their  existence  oh god she will die the gods of the mcu  are clearly not determined by worship or  by power otherwise the reality rewriting  scarlet witch would be one of their  greatest  so far we've covered the fact that mcu's  humans are stronger than ever both in  the growing number of superpowered  beings in their ranks and in the  hyper-advanced technology they either  have or will soon have we've also  covered the fact that the mcu's god  seemed to merely be a loose assortment  of powerful beings that aren't powerful  enough to be considered abstract  entities like eternity or the watcher  the obvious question then is what  exactly does the cosmic hierarchy of the  mcu look like two celestials attended  zeus's meeting of the gods even though  they aren't gods themselves every god  and their uncle seems to be aware of  eternity and so presumably also its twin  infinity do universal constants and  abstract beings cohabitate freely with  gods we know gods do with people so that  implies a surprisingly open and tolerant  universal community do the other  universal constants that we haven't met  like galactus and the phoenix act in a  similarly casual manner and we've met  two elders of the universe so far the  collector and the grand master so are  they gods abstract beans or something  else it's surprising that a universe so  jam-packed with near-omnipotent super  beans of varying backgrounds hasn't  destroyed itself yet  one of the biggest reveals in thor love  and thunder is at valhalla the fabled  resting place for deceased asgardian  gods is real upon her death foster is  welcomed there by heimdall who looks  happy healthy and serene so if heimdall  is in valhalla is odin there too what  about other fallen asgardian warriors  and if foster is welcomed there because  she proved herself worthy and wielded  mjolnir in defense of asgard then surely  everyone's favorite mjolnir wielder  captain america could end up there as  well perhaps more important than any of  these questions is the matter of  valhalla's accessibility for one thing  the very job definition of a valkyrie is  to freely travel between valhalla and  the other realms so can king valkyrie  just pop in to visit foster and heimdall  can she relay messages between them and  the living can she bring them back  alongside valhalla the mcu has already  confirmed the existence of two other  afterlifes the ancestral plane of the  wakandan people and the duat of the  egyptian people unless marvel plans to  make the exceedingly bold claim that  those three religions are the only three  that got it right then in general we now  know that afterlives exist and seemingly  all of them  the dead will drag you down into the  duet where you will remain forever  frozen in sand  as we mentioned steve rogers wielded  mjolnir so will he be given a choice  between valhalla and a christian heaven  tony stark saved half the sentient life  in the galaxy but died apparently as a  non-believer will he spend an eternity  of punishment in some version of hell  as tricky as it would be to stick the  landing on this existential conundrum an  answer from marvel would be a major  relief when it comes to the fate of some  fan favorite characters  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]

